"""
## Purpose and audience
For the Engine2D Vulkan maintainer landing the EXACT-SIZE image staging array,
the byte-path half of
`doc/08_tracking/bug/vulkan_image_upload_scratch_double_pack_2026-09-12.md`.

The byte lane packs a `[u32]` image into `self.image_upload_scratch` and uploads
the initialized prefix. That scratch used to be grown to a HIGH-WATER MARK and
never shrunk, while `vulkan_sffi_copy_to_buffer_prefix` admits a prefix on the
interpreter array ABI only when `byte_count == data.len()`. So after one large
image, every smaller composite was refused the prefix and packed the WHOLE image
a SECOND time through `_pixels_to_bytes`. Measured at 900x760: 262 of 264
composites paid that second pack, 181 s of an 830 s frame. Sizing the scratch to
exactly `byte_count` makes the admission test hold for every size.

## Operator workflow
`SIMPLE_LIB=src SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE=interpreter SIMPLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=0 \
 <vulkan-capable simple> run \
 test/01_unit/lib/gc_async_mut/gpu/engine2d/backend_vulkan_image_exact_scratch_spec.spl`

Run it with `SIMPLE_VK_IMAGE_UPLOAD` UNSET. This file is about the DEFAULT byte
path deliberately: the typed `[u32]` lane returns before `_prepare_image_upload`
is ever reached, so under `SIMPLE_VK_IMAGE_UPLOAD=u32` the counters this file
asserts on are all zero and the defect is unobservable. That is precisely why
the byte path needs its own guard -- it is what every binary that predates
`rt_vulkan_copy_to_buffer_u32` runs, and what this one runs by default.

## Compatibility and limitations
REQUIRES a real Vulkan device and says so by FAILING, never skipping: the first
expectation asserts `backend_name() == "vulkan"`. A CPU fallback paints correct
pixels through `emu_draw_image_blend` without touching the staging array at all,
so a skip would make every counter below vacuously zero and the file would pass
while testing nothing.

The counters are PROCESS-WIDE module globals, not per-engine fields, because the
oracle is read through the engine facade which does not expose the backend
struct. Each example therefore calls `vulkan_image_pack_evidence_reset()` first
and must not be reordered into one shared engine.

## Verification guidance and troubleshooting
The load-bearing oracle is a COUNTER, not a pixel: the two packs write identical
bytes, so no pixel can witness the second one. `byte_fallbacks` counts entries to
the exact-size `_pixels_to_bytes` path -- the second walk -- and must be 0 for
every size an admitted composite uses. `packs` counts walks of the staging array,
so "one large then one small image" must read exactly `packs=2`.

The sabotage that proves it discriminates: restore the high-water test in
`_prepare_image_upload` (`!=` back to `<`). The small-after-large example then
reads `packs=2 byte_fallbacks=1` and fails on the fallback count, while every
pixel expectation in this file stays GREEN -- which is the whole point.
"""
# Lifecycle: doc/08_tracking/bug/vulkan_image_upload_scratch_double_pack_2026-09-12.md
# @manual_section: vulkan_image_exact_scratch
# @cover src/lib/gc_async_mut/gpu/engine2d/backend_vulkan_helpers.spl 3%
# @cover src/lib/gc_async_mut/gpu/engine2d/backend_vulkan.spl 2%
# @tag: unit, gpu, engine2d, vulkan, image, perf

use std.spec.*
use std.gc_async_mut.gpu.engine2d.engine.{Engine2D}

val FB: i32 = 96
val BIG: i32 = 40
val SMALL: i32 = 4
val BIG_AT: i32 = 2
val SMALL_AT: i32 = 60
val GROUND: u32 = 0xFF101010u32

fn image_color(k: i64) -> u32:
    """Oracle colour for source pixel k, computed without the renderer.

    Every pixel differs in all three channels so a channel swap, a big-endian
    widening and a dropped element stay three distinguishable failures. Alpha is
    opaque: a translucent source would blend against the ground and stop being
    an exact oracle.
    """
    (4278190080 + (k + 1) * 0x0F0703).to_i32().to_u32()

fn oracle_image(side: i32) -> [u32]:
    var out: [u32] = [0u32; side.to_i64() * side.to_i64()]
    var k: i64 = 0
    while k < out.len().to_i64():
        out[k] = image_color(k)
        k = k + 1
    out

fn dst_index(k: i64, side: i32, at_x: i32, at_y: i32) -> i64:
    val sx = k % side.to_i64()
    val sy = k / side.to_i64()
    (at_y.to_i64() + sy) * FB.to_i64() + at_x.to_i64() + sx

describe "The byte image lane packs each composite exactly once, whatever preceded it":

    it "runs on a real Vulkan device, and fails rather than skipping when it cannot":
        # PRECONDITION. Every claim below is about the DEVICE upload path. The
        # host fallback never allocates a staging array, so on CPU every counter
        # would read zero and this file would pass without exercising anything.
        val e = Engine2D.create_with_backend_fast(FB, FB, "vulkan")
        val name = e.backend_name()
        e.shutdown()
        expect(name).to_equal("vulkan")

    it "packs a single composite exactly once":
        # BASELINE. Establishes what "once" reads as, so the small-after-large
        # example below cannot be satisfied by a counter that simply never moves.
        val e = Engine2D.create_with_backend_fast(FB, FB, "vulkan")
        e.vulkan_image_pack_evidence_reset()
        e.clear(GROUND)
        e.draw_image(SMALL_AT, SMALL_AT, SMALL, SMALL, oracle_image(SMALL))
        e.finalize_compute_frame_no_readback()
        val evidence = e.vulkan_image_pack_evidence()
        e.shutdown()
        expect(evidence).to_equal("packs=1 byte_fallbacks=0 scratch_resizes=1")

    it "packs a SMALLER image after a larger one exactly once, not twice":
        # REPRODUCING. This is the defect, in its minimal shape: the 40x40
        # composite grows the staging array, then the 4x4 composite needs a
        # SHORTER prefix. Under the high-water scratch the prefix facade
        # declined and `_pixels_to_bytes` walked the image a second time, so
        # this read `byte_fallbacks=1`. Both numbers are absolute, not a
        # threshold: two composites, two packs, no fallback.
        val e = Engine2D.create_with_backend_fast(FB, FB, "vulkan")
        e.vulkan_image_pack_evidence_reset()
        e.clear(GROUND)
        e.draw_image(BIG_AT, BIG_AT, BIG, BIG, oracle_image(BIG))
        e.draw_image(SMALL_AT, SMALL_AT, SMALL, SMALL, oracle_image(SMALL))
        e.finalize_compute_frame_no_readback()
        val evidence = e.vulkan_image_pack_evidence()
        e.shutdown()
        expect(evidence).to_equal("packs=2 byte_fallbacks=0 scratch_resizes=2")

    it "still paints the smaller image's known pixels after the larger one":
        # CORRECTNESS. The counter oracle above would also be satisfied by a
        # change that simply stopped uploading, so the pixels are checked in the
        # same order the counters were. 16 independent point checks: a
        # byte-swapped widening, an off-by-one stride and a truncated payload
        # each fail in a different, readable pattern.
        val e = Engine2D.create_with_backend_fast(FB, FB, "vulkan")
        e.clear(GROUND)
        e.draw_image(BIG_AT, BIG_AT, BIG, BIG, oracle_image(BIG))
        e.draw_image(SMALL_AT, SMALL_AT, SMALL, SMALL, oracle_image(SMALL))
        e.finalize_compute_frame_no_readback()
        val px = e.read_pixels_with_source().pixels
        e.shutdown()
        var wrong: i64 = 0
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < SMALL.to_i64() * SMALL.to_i64():
            if px[dst_index(k, SMALL, SMALL_AT, SMALL_AT)] != image_color(k):
                wrong = wrong + 1
            k = k + 1
        expect(wrong).to_equal(0)

    it "still paints the LARGER image's known pixels, so the shrink did not truncate it":
        # BOUNDARY, in the other direction. Re-sizing the scratch DOWN for the
        # small image must not affect a later large one: the next 40x40 upload
        # has to grow it back rather than upload a 4x4-sized prefix. Checking
        # the large image last is what makes that observable.
        val e = Engine2D.create_with_backend_fast(FB, FB, "vulkan")
        e.clear(GROUND)
        e.draw_image(SMALL_AT, SMALL_AT, SMALL, SMALL, oracle_image(SMALL))
        e.draw_image(BIG_AT, BIG_AT, BIG, BIG, oracle_image(BIG))
        e.finalize_compute_frame_no_readback()
        val px = e.read_pixels_with_source().pixels
        e.shutdown()
        var wrong: i64 = 0
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < BIG.to_i64() * BIG.to_i64():
            if px[dst_index(k, BIG, BIG_AT, BIG_AT)] != image_color(k):
                wrong = wrong + 1
            k = k + 1
        expect(wrong).to_equal(0)

    it "repeats the SAME size without re-allocating the staging array":
        # REUSE SURVIVES. The fix trades a high-water array for an exact-size
        # one, so the obvious worry is that it now allocates on every composite.
        # Three same-size uploads must resize exactly ONCE -- the reuse that
        # mattered is intact, and only a size CHANGE costs an allocation.
        val e = Engine2D.create_with_backend_fast(FB, FB, "vulkan")
        e.vulkan_image_pack_evidence_reset()
        e.clear(GROUND)
        e.draw_image(SMALL_AT, SMALL_AT, SMALL, SMALL, oracle_image(SMALL))
        e.draw_image(SMALL_AT, 10i32, SMALL, SMALL, oracle_image(SMALL))
        e.draw_image(SMALL_AT, 20i32, SMALL, SMALL, oracle_image(SMALL))
        e.finalize_compute_frame_no_readback()
        val evidence = e.vulkan_image_pack_evidence()
        e.shutdown()
        expect(evidence).to_equal("packs=3 byte_fallbacks=0 scratch_resizes=1")
